WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.680
Your cortex is only about twice as thick as a mouse's.

2
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:10.300
So why is its surface roughly a thousand times larger?

3
00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:13.093
Almost all of the gain is sideways.

4
00:00:14.173 --> 00:00:16.013
How does a brain build more of the same wall?

5
00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:18.557
Compare two kinds of division.

6
00:00:19.617 --> 00:00:23.337
In a symmetric proliferative division, one progenitor makes two.

7
00:00:24.137 --> 00:00:26.897
If every progenitor does this once, the pool doubles.

8
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.800
In an asymmetric neurogenic division, it makes one progenitor and one neuron.

9
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.060
The pool stays the same size.

10
00:00:36.083 --> 00:00:40.883
The radial glia lining the ventricle, the founders, divide symmetrically first.

11
00:00:41.943 --> 00:00:43.223
Later they switch to making neurons.

12
00:00:44.283 --> 00:00:48.003
Pasko Rakic showed that young neurons climb radial glial fibres.

13
00:00:49.023 --> 00:00:50.663
He argued that they stack into columns.

14
00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:55.323
In 1988, he proposed the radial unit hypothesis.

15
00:00:56.383 --> 00:01:00.683
If every unit takes up about the same patch of surface, the number of units sets the

16
00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:03.523
area. The neurons per unit set the thickness.

17
00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:10.347
Predict. Species B gets three extra rounds of symmetric division, before the switch.

18
00:01:11.407 --> 00:01:16.327
Species C gets three extra asymmetric divisions per unit, after it, each adding one

19
00:01:16.327 --> 00:01:17.327
neuron.

20
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.530
Which species gains more neurons?

21
00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:21.590
And which one gains area?

22
00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:36.973
Species B. Doubling three times gives eight times the units, so, with the same patch

23
00:01:36.973 --> 00:01:38.713
per unit, eight times the area.

24
00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:45.067
Species C gains about three percent more neurons, and no extra units, so no extra

25
00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:46.067
area.

26
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.500
Doubling multiplies.

27
00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:53.220
Adding only adds.

28
00:01:54.033 --> 00:01:55.173
Back to the mouse.

29
00:01:56.193 --> 00:01:58.073
Ten doublings make about a thousandfold.

30
00:01:58.973 --> 00:02:03.813
So, in this model, with every unit the same size, ten extra rounds would close the

31
00:02:03.813 --> 00:02:04.813
gap.

32
00:02:05.017 --> 00:02:07.897
Our brain is about three times a chimpanzee's.

33
00:02:08.317 --> 00:02:12.777
If our cortical area differs by a similar factor, the gap is under two rounds.

34
00:02:13.817 --> 00:02:17.557
Rakic made a related argument for primates in 1995.

35
00:02:18.697 --> 00:02:22.957
A delay of a few cell cycles before the switch could greatly enlarge the cortex.

36
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:28.980
In mice, a stabilized form of beta β-catenin kept more progenitors dividing.

37
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.500
The cortex grew in area, and buckled into folds.

38
00:02:33.583 --> 00:02:38.383
That shows extra founder divisions can enlarge the sheet, not how primates did it.

39
00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:44.087
In organoids, human cells took longer than ape cells to become radial glia.

40
00:02:44.967 --> 00:02:46.567
The human organoids grew larger.

41
00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:50.207
Neither test was done in a developing human brain.

42
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.680
Multiply, don't add.

43
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.100
Extra rounds of doubling can multiply the neurons a cortex makes.

44
00:02:58.133 --> 00:03:03.053
That is the leading explanation for our big cortex, and nearly all of the gain went

45
00:03:03.053 --> 00:03:04.053
into area.

46
00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:07.957
So for every cell and gene that follows, ask what it changes.
